From ac74591d05e11b7a48743723134f15eff58502c9 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Kuzma30 <kuzemkoa@gmail.com>
Date: Mon, 26 Oct 2026 10:09:17 +0200
Subject: [PATCH 12/25] Modeling Algorithms - Close a fillet ending next to a
 fillet of the same radius

In the FreeCAD screw, the root of the thread starts at the bottom face next
to a fillet of radius 1 along the edge between the start wall and the upper
flank, and the start wall ends in cusps where the fillets of the crests
are tangent to the outer cylinder. The fillets of the root and of the wall
failed there ("command not done", faulty vertices) or gave invalid shapes
for most radii.

- At an end of the spine, after one side has left its face (path on the
  tangent plane) and the path of the other side is lost on the face beyond
  a tangent edge, the path is computed again on the extended surfaces of
  the two faces of the last surfdata (PerformSetOfSurfOnElSpine). This
  surfdata is kept with both sides out of the faces. SearchFace and IsObst
  skip the limits of these extended surfaces.
- PerformSetOfSurfOnElSpine also goes on after a surfdata whose side has
  left its face when the other side ends on a tangent edge, the first side
  staying on the same extension, unless the faces beyond the two sides
  have one surface (the end is then cut by that surface, as in
  PerformCapAfterExtension).
- ChFi3d_BuildPlane extends a BSpline face instead of taking its tangent
  plane for the path out of the face: the fillet stays close to the one on
  the face (deviation from 1.4e-3 down to 1e-4 here).
- PerformCapAtMitre closes the end where the side that left its face
  across a sharp edge gives the face beyond (the bottom face), extended up
  to the fillet, and the other side gives the face beyond the tangent edge
  (the fillet of the wall), trimmed by the fillet: a cut along the first
  from the point where the side left its face, across the section between
  the last two surfdatas, to the point where it crosses the common edge of
  the two faces, and along the second (a mitre) from the end of the other
  side to the same point.
- PerformCapOnOneFace closes the end when the faces beyond the edges left
  by the two sides are one face, the edge of the second side being sharp
  or smooth (the cusps of the wall): the fillet is cut along that face
  from the point where the first side left its face, across the sections
  between the surfdatas, to the end of the other side. The face is
  extended up to the cut or trimmed by it, depending on whether the part
  between the cut and the vertex lies out of the face; probes classified
  the same on both sides of the cut decide first (where the cut runs close
  to an edge of the face, a probe crosses it). The orientations of the
  surfdatas after the split one are taken from it by their normals at the
  sections, on the parameters of their traces.
- When the last surfdata itself goes on after its side left the face (the
  usual end on a face), its orientation is checked against the surfdata
  before it: computed partly out of its faces, it may be wrong.
- The intersections of a fillet with a surface computed by
  ChFi3d_ComputeCurves may stay far from the surfaces on the extended
  fillet: when their tolerance exceeds twice the approximation tolerance,
  their points are brought onto both surfaces and the curve is
  interpolated on the fillet.

The comparison of the orientations of two surfdatas by their normals is
ChFi3d_OrientAsNeighbour.

Tests bugs/modalg_8/fillet_root_mitre_at_wall_fillet,
fillet_root_rolling_on_wall_fillet, fillet_root_small_radius_at_bottom
and fillet_wall_profile_cusp_ends (data file
freecad_screw_wall_profile.brep).

